 { Targeting weaknesses lies at the heart of positional play. The ability to quickly identify and exploit these features of the position will mark the first step to victory in many of your positional battles at the chessboard. In chess, the term 'weakness' is used frequently, and can have different meanings depending on the context. When attacking, you may hear a term like back rank weakness to refer to a vulnerable king along the opponent’s first rank. In opening play, some players may describe themselves as having a weakness in their repertoire. Even non-material aspects of play, such as a player having a psychological weakness, are often topics of discussion. In this chapter we are interested in structural weaknesses, namely ‘weak squares’. A weak square is defined as one that cannot be defended by a pawn, making it vulnerable to enemy occupation, either immediately or in the future. Consider the position we see here. } 1. { -KEY- } -- { Material is equal, yet Black has the much superior endgame. On the surface level, the reason is because they have the more active pieces. But why has Black been able to find such good squares for their pieces? The answer has to do with weaknesses in the White pawn structure. The e4 and d5 squares are weak squares. Since they cannot be protected by a friendly pawn, the Black player has been able to take control of these points and use them as entry points into the White position. This chapter is of special importance, since other strategies or techniques explored in this course will build upon the understanding of weak squares. The material is divided into four sections: (1) The role of weak squares. (2) How to target and exploit weak squares. (3) How to create and defend weak squares (4) The concept of color complexes, where an entire set of squares becomes weak. By the end of this chapter, you will be better able to identify and make use of important weak squares in the position for the purpose of gaining the advantage. At the same time, you’ll also be better equipped to prevent or to manage these possibilities when you find yourself on the defending side. A closing remark before we begin: In this chapter, our exploration centers on weak squares, setting aside the topic of weak pawns for now. The study of weak pawns, including specific formations like isolated pawns, will be addressed in later chapters. Let's begin! } 
